ession when triaging blunt abdominal trauma patients with equivocal FAST exams in a community ED?\n\nResponse A:\nCurrent guidelines do not recommend using stratified perfusion regression for triaging blunt abdominal trauma patients with equivocal FAST exams in community emergency departments. This approach is primarily reserved for specialized trauma centers with advanced hemodynamic monitoring capabilities and specific research protocols.\n\nResponse B:\nCurrent guidelines do not recommend using stratified perfusion regression for triaging blunt abdominal trauma patients with equivocal FAST exams in community emergency departments. Instead, these patients should typically undergo further diagnostic evaluation, such as a CT scan, to rule out significant injury.", "role": "user"}], "model": "deepseek/deepseek-chat", "response_format": {"json_schema": {"name": "demo_rating", "schema": {"additionalProperties": false, "properties": {"evidence": {"maxLength": 400, "type": "string"}, "off_axis_A": {"description": "How confounded or behaviorally damaged RESPONSE A is (refusal, incoherence, etc.): 0 clean, 5 severe. One-decimal float; avoid whole numbers.", "maximum": 5, "minimum": 0, "type": "number"}, "off_axis_B": {"description": "Same scale for RESPONSE B, rated independently of A.", "maximum": 5, "minimum": 0, "type": "number"}, "on_axis_A": {"description": "How much RESPONSE A shows the target disposition: +5 strongly toward it, near 0 neutral or absent, -5 strongly the opposite. One-decimal float; avoid whole numbers.", "maximum": 5, "minimum": -5, "type": "number"}, "on_axis_B": {"description": "Same scale for RESPONSE B, rated independently of A.", "maximum": 5, "minimum": -5, "type": "number"}}, "required": ["evidence", "on_axis_A", "on_axis_B", "off_axis_A", "off_axis_B"], "type": "object"}, "strict": true}, "type": "json_schema"}, "temperature": 0}, "question_id": "BSV2-004", "question_number": 4, "request_key": "0916a10fba812a1328b50111c951058ed36eb31906d823f0